artifact.self_contained_problem:
Consider a 3D bipedal walking robot modeled as a hybrid dynamical system whose continuous phase is governed by Lagrangian dynamics with configuration q in a smooth manifold Q and control input u (joint torques), and whose discrete phase models foot-ground impact via a reset map. A periodic walking gait is a periodic orbit of the closed-loop hybrid system under a feedback controller. Two cost functionals are commonly used to design such controllers by minimizing them over admissible periodic gaits:

(i) Integral-squared torque per step length: J_1 = (1/L) ∫_0^T ||u(t)||^2 dt, where T is the step duration and L the step length.
(ii) Specific cost of transport: J_2 = (∫_0^T P_in(t) dt)/(m g L), where P_in is the input power, m the total mass, and g gravitational acceleration.

Let H denote a reference set of measured human walking gaits (e.g., trajectories of joint angles, ground reaction forces, and center-of-mass motion recorded from healthy human subjects). Define a notion of distance d(γ, H) between a candidate periodic robot gait γ and the reference set H (for instance, a metric on trajectory spaces or on suitable kinematic/dynamic features).

Problem: Construct a cost functional J: {admissible periodic gaits} → ℝ, distinct from J_1 and J_2 and explicitly incorporating a distance d(·, H) from human walking, with the property that the minimizers of J over admissible periodic gaits of the bipedal hybrid model are periodic gaits γ* that are quantifiably closer to H (under d) than the minimizers of J_1 or J_2. Provide:

(a) a precise definition of the metric d on the relevant trajectory/feature space;
(b) the explicit form of J;
(c) a proof or constructive argument that minimizing J yields stable periodic gaits of the hybrid system;
(d) a comparison establishing that minimizers of J are closer to human walking (in d) than minimizers of J_1 and J_2.
